description: TravelIsrael data model, enums, and access control
alwaysApply: true
---
# Entities
profiles, regions, places, trails, trips, trip_stations, ratings, comments, images, favorites, reports, app_config

# Key Enums
- Status: draft | pending | approved | rejected
- Difficulty: easy | medium | hard
- StationType: place | trail
- UserRole: guest | user | admin
- EntityType: trip | place | trail

# Trip Computed Fields
total_duration_minutes, total_distance_km, estimated_price, difficulty, accessibility, region, station_count

# Access Control
Enforced by RLS in Postgres. lib/data/ mirrors the same rules so the UI can
hide what the DB would reject — never as the only line of defense.
- guest: read approved content only
- user: CRUD own content, ratings, comments, favorites
- admin: full access, approve/reject content, manage reports

# Status Flow
draft → pending → approved
                → rejected → (edit) → pending

Owners may edit only while pending or rejected, and every owner edit returns
the row to pending. Only an admin can set approved.

# Schemas
Two schemas in one Supabase project, switched by NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_SCHEMA:
- public — the real environment
- demo — full mirror with rich test data; shares auth.users and Storage
Baseline DDL and demo seed live in supabase/. See supabase/README.md.

# RPCs
resolve_bootstrap_role, sync_bootstrap_admin, admin_update_user_role,
approve_trip_cascade — SECURITY DEFINER, each re-checks is_admin() internally.
